oney": 0.62}, "rating_margin": 20.0, "sigma_race": 6.0, "sigma_total": 6.52, "baseline_margin": 21.06, "analyst_adjustment": -0.06, "key_factors": ["D+11 district with a long-tenured incumbent seeking another term", "Late UNH poll of likely voters shows Pingree up 22 points", "Huge fundraising and cash gap favoring Pingree", "Prediction markets price a Democratic win near certain"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point strongly to Pingree: a D+11 seat, incumbency, a 22-point likely-voter lead in the September UNH poll, and a lopsided money advantage over Russell, who has under $10,000 on hand. Markets price her at roughly 98%, consistent with a Safe D rating.", "watch": "A late polling shift or heavy Republican outside spending could narrow the margin, though nothing in the current evidence suggests a competitive race.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["No expert rater lists this race; treated as safe for the party holding the seat.", "Incumbent Chellie Pingree (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Chellie Pingree seeks another term in a Portland-area district that leans Democratic by about 11 points. The only public poll, a University of New Hampshire survey of likely voters from late September, shows her leading Ronald Russell 57 to 35, a 22-point margin. Russell, running a rematch campaign, has raised modestly and holds little cash, while Pingree has out-raised him and faces no outside opposition spending.\n\nPrediction markets price a Pingree win at roughly 98 percent, and no expert raters flag the race as competitive. The final debate covered healthcare, Iran, and ICE without producing signs of a shift. Everything points to a comfortable incumbent win, with the main uncertainty being the size of the margin rather than the outcome.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.989, "margin": 20.81}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9938, "margin": 20.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9879, "margin": 20.9}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9946, "margin": 21.5